{
  "description": "Language-agnostic vectors of the minimum feature width measured on the VISIBLE part of a filled shape (paper millimetres, minFeature 0.2 mm). `covers` are the closed filled shapes painted over the shape (higher in depth order), each with the pen of its outline; the visible part is the shape minus the union of the covers grown by half their pen (the outline inks that much paper). `widthMm: null` means nothing is visible, so the shape contributes nothing to the print check. Hand-computed: a 1.0 by 0.1 mm bar completely inside a body is hidden (null); a 2 by 1 mm rectangle whose left 1.9 mm are covered keeps a 0.1 mm wide visible strip (width 0.1, area 0.1 mm2 above the tolerance 0.01 mm2); a 2 by 0.1 mm bar covered over x 0..1 keeps a 1 by 0.1 mm strip (0.1); a 2 by 1 mm rectangle covered over x 0..1 keeps a 1 by 1 mm square (1.0); two bodies at x 0..0.44 and 0.56..1.0 leave a 0.12 mm wide channel of a 1 mm square (0.12); with a 0.1 mm pen a 1.0 by 0.1 mm bar covered up to x = 0.95 leaves 0.05 mm of paper, exactly the half pen inked by the outline of the cover, so nothing is visible (null); a 2 by 1 mm rectangle covered up to x = 1.7 with a 0.1 mm pen keeps the strip x 1.75..2.0 (0.25). A 1.0 by 0.1 mm bar covered up to x = 0.995 leaves a 0.005 by 0.1 mm piece of 0.0005 mm2, below the tolerance (minFeature/2)2 = 0.01 mm2: residue of coincident edges, not a drawn part (null). Without covers the measure equals the plain feature width. Widening a shape widens its visible width: bars of 0.06, 0.09, 0.15, 0.18 and 0.3 mm width, their left half covered by a body drawn with a 0.1 mm pen, measure exactly their own width and grow strictly.",
